# Website UI/UX Improvements and Layout Refinements

## General Instructions

Improve the existing website by implementing the design refinements and functional fixes described below. The objective is to create a more polished, professional, and user-friendly experience while preserving the existing brand identity.

Before making changes, inspect the relevant files, components, layouts, and existing behavior to understand the current implementation.

**Important requirements:**
- Preserve the existing sitemap, content, and overall navigation structure.
- Maintain the current brand identity, typography, and color palette.
- Focus on improving spacing, visual hierarchy, layout efficiency, and interaction behavior.
- Ensure all changes work correctly on desktop, tablet, and mobile.
- Fix underlying functional issues rather than applying superficial styling workarounds.
- Avoid unnecessary whitespace, oversized sections, and layouts that force visitors to scroll excessively.
- Reuse existing components and styling conventions wherever possible.

---

## 1. Global Navigation Bar

### Remove the Contact Link
- Remove the **"Contact"** link from the navigation bar to avoid duplication with the existing quote-request or contact CTA.
- Preserve the remaining navigation items and their existing destinations.
- Adjust the spacing and alignment of the navbar to maintain a balanced appearance after removing the link.

### Fix Mobile Navigation Behavior
- Currently, the mobile navigation menu opens or becomes visible again whenever the visitor navigates to another page.
- Fix this behavior so that the mobile menu closes automatically after the visitor selects a navigation link.
- Ensure that navigating between pages does not leave the menu unexpectedly open or display an awkward transition.
- Preserve the existing mobile menu design and opening/closing interactions.
- Verify that the behavior works consistently across all pages.

---

## 2. Homepage (`index.html`)

### Hero Section
- Reduce the overall height of the hero section to create a more compact and balanced first impression.
- Move the hero image and the accompanying content slightly upward, including the description and call-to-action (CTA).
- Eliminate unnecessary vertical space while preserving the visual impact of the hero.
- Ensure that the headline, description, image, and CTA remain properly aligned and readable across different screen sizes.
- Maintain a balanced composition on both desktop and mobile.

### Manifest Section
- Remove the animated or moving icons currently displayed in the bottom-left area of the Manifest section.
- Ensure that removing these icons does not affect the surrounding layout, spacing, or other section elements.
- Clean up any unnecessary spacing left behind after their removal.

### "5 étapes, un seul..." Section
- Reduce the excessive whitespace below the **"5 étapes, un seul..."** section.
- Adjust the bottom margin and padding to create a more natural transition into the following section.
- Preserve sufficient spacing for readability and visual separation.

### Testimonial Section and Final CTA
- Remove the excessive whitespace above the CTA section containing the headline:

  **"Vous avez une cuisine à transformer ?"**

- Reduce the top margin or padding as appropriate so that the CTA feels visually connected to the preceding testimonial section.
- Improve the CTA button's text contrast. The current light-green background combined with white text does not provide a sufficiently clear visual result.
- Change the CTA text color to a dark, readable color that contrasts appropriately with the light-green background.
- Preserve the existing button styling and brand colors wherever possible, changing only what is necessary to improve readability.
- Verify that the CTA remains accessible and visually consistent across desktop and mobile.

---

## 3. Expertise Page (`expertise.html`)

### Fix Sticky Tabs and Navbar Alignment
- The expertise tabs become sticky while scrolling, but there is currently an unwanted gap between the sticky tabs and the sticky navigation bar when the tabs reach their pinned position.
- Fix the sticky positioning so that the tabs sit directly beneath the navbar without an unnecessary gap.
- Account for the navbar's actual height, including any changes in height or styling when it becomes sticky.
- Avoid overlapping content, layout jumps, or inconsistent positioning during scrolling.
- Ensure the solution works correctly across desktop, tablet, and mobile layouts.

### Highlight the Active Tab
- When a visitor scrolls to an expertise section or selects its corresponding tab, highlight the relevant tab using the website's brand color.
- Ensure that the active state updates correctly as the visitor moves between sections.
- Apply consistent styling to the active tab, such as brand-colored text, an underline, or another existing design-system indicator.
- Keep inactive tabs visually distinct from the active tab.
- If the tabs support click-based navigation, preserve their functionality and ensure that clicking a tab scrolls to the correct section.
- Account for the sticky navbar and tabs when calculating scroll positions so that section headings remain visible rather than being hidden behind sticky elements.

---

## 4. Projects Overview Page

### Improve the Overall Layout
- Rework the project listing layout to make it more visually appealing, organized, and space-efficient.
- The current layout stacks too many elements vertically, causing visitors to scroll excessively before discovering other projects.
- Reduce the amount of vertical space occupied by individual project entries.
- Improve the relationship between project images, titles, descriptions, metadata, and navigation elements.
- Use a more compact and visually structured layout, such as a responsive grid or carefully designed project cards, where appropriate for the existing design.
- Maintain sufficient whitespace for readability without allowing individual projects to dominate the page unnecessarily.
- Ensure that visitors can scan the portfolio quickly and discover multiple projects with less scrolling.

### Fix Horizontal Alignment and Empty Space
- The current desktop layout leaves a large amount of unused space on the right side of the projects page.
- Rework the content container and project layout so that the available horizontal space is used more effectively.
- Center the main project content within the available desktop viewport and reduce unnecessary empty space on either side.
- Adjust the maximum container width, grid columns, image sizing, and alignment as needed.
- Avoid simply stretching individual images or text blocks to fill the screen. Maintain a balanced composition with appropriate proportions.
- Preserve the existing responsive behavior and ensure the layout adapts naturally to smaller screens.

### Visual Consistency
- Maintain consistent spacing, image proportions, card dimensions, and typography across the project listing.
- Ensure that project images and associated text remain visually connected.
- Preserve existing project content, links, and navigation functionality.

---

## 5. Project Detail Page

### Add an "Après" Section
- The project detail page currently includes an **"Avant"** section but does not provide a corresponding **"Après"** section.
- Add a dedicated "Après" section to showcase the completed kitchen or renovation result.
- Maintain a clear visual distinction between the before and after states.
- Position the two sections logically so visitors can easily compare the original space with the completed project.
- Reuse existing project data and image assets where available. If the required after images are missing, identify the missing assets rather than inventing project-specific results.

### Redesign the Before-and-After Presentation
- Improve the layout of both the "Avant" and "Après" sections.
- Present the images using a polished gallery-style interface instead of large, vertically stacked image blocks.
- Where appropriate, use a responsive image grid, gallery, or lightbox-style interface to make the images easier to browse.
- Ensure that visitors can view multiple images without excessive scrolling.
- Keep the "Avant" and "Après" labels clearly visible and consistently styled.
- Maintain consistent image ratios, spacing, cropping, and alignment.
- If both states contain multiple images, make it easy to distinguish which images belong to the before gallery and which belong to the after gallery.
- Ensure that any gallery interactions, such as opening an image in a larger view or navigating between images, work correctly if implemented.

### Responsive Behavior
- On desktop, use the available horizontal space to create an attractive gallery layout.
- On mobile, adapt the gallery to a suitable single-column or compact grid layout without horizontal overflow.
- Ensure image controls and gallery navigation remain usable on touchscreens.

---

## 6. Responsive Design and Regression Checks

After implementing the changes, verify the website at desktop, tablet, and mobile breakpoints.

Pay particular attention to:
- Navbar and mobile menu behavior during page navigation.
- Hero section height and content positioning.
- Sticky navbar and expertise tab alignment.
- Active tab highlighting during scrolling.
- Project overview density and horizontal alignment.
- Before-and-after galleries on project detail pages.
- CTA text contrast and section spacing.
- Image sizing, page overflow, and unexpected layout shifts.

